# GNOME Shell extension conventions

`best-practices.md` in the repository root is the upstream GNOME/EGO guidance for
AI-generated extensions and overrides general JavaScript habits. Read it before
changing extension code.

## Target exactly GNOME Shell 49

No compatibility shims, no `typeof x === 'function'` guards, no optional chaining
on values that cannot be null. Use `TextDecoder`, `console.error` and ESM
directly.

## Process isolation

- `extension.js` and `shell/` run in GNOME Shell.
- `prefs.js` runs in a separate preferences process.
- `lib/` is shared and must **never** import `St`, `Clutter`, `Gtk`, `Gdk` or `Adw`.

Keep `extension.js` limited to `enable()`/`disable()`, kept adjacent, with new
logic going into a module.

## Resource ownership

A class cleans up whatever it created, in `destroy()`, in this order: GLib
sources, signal handlers, child references, then `super.destroy()` last. Never
guard with a `this._destroyed` flag; drop the reference after destroying.

```js
destroy() {
    this._monitor.disconnect(this._changedId);
    this._settings.disconnect(this._settingsChangedId);
    super.destroy();
}
```

## Settings

The schema ID lives in `metadata.json`, so always use `this.getSettings()`
without arguments. Run `make schemas` after editing the schema, or GNOME Shell
will read stale defaults.

## Mount state

Never cache whether a remote is mounted. Derive it from `/proc/self/mounts` and
refresh on `Gio.UnixMountMonitor::mounts-changed`, so mounts created outside the
extension are handled correctly.

## Comments

Only document constraints the code cannot express, such as why `--daemon` is used
or why an unmount needs a lazy fallback. No line-by-line narration.
